Risk Assessment.content:
{
  "score": 28,
  "score_rationale": "CORZ carries extreme financial risk driven by $5.39B total debt (including a post-Q1 $3.30B secured note issuance), a $990M short-term debt reclassification with unresolved refinancing terms, persistent negative FCF of ~$140M/quarter despite rising CapEx, and near-total revenue concentration in a single counterparty ($CRWV) — all amplified by a beta of 5.45 and BTC price sensitivity that makes operating cash flow highly volatile.",
  "key_findings": [
    {
      "finding": "Post-Q1 2026 total debt exploded to approximately $5.39B after Core Scientific Finance issued $3.30B in Secured Notes in May 2026, layered onto existing $2.09B in convertible notes, creating a leverage profile that is existentially sensitive to capital market conditions.",
      "evidence": "[Source: FMP /balance-sheet] Q1 2026 LT Debt $1.06B + ST Debt $0.99B; [Source: SEC 10-Q 2026-05-06 Item 1A] '$3.30 billion aggregate principal amount of Secured Notes' issued May 2026 + '$2.09 billion aggregate principal amount of indebtedness' as of March 31, 2026"
    },
    {
      "finding": "The $990M reclassification to short-term debt in Q1 2026 (from zero ST debt in prior quarters) collapsed the current ratio to 0.55 from 1.15, signaling either a covenant trigger, maturity within 12 months, or conversion feature activation — the precise terms remain undisclosed in XBRL and SEC excerpts.",
      "evidence": "[Source: FMP /balance-sheet] Q1 2026: ST Debt $0.99B, Current Ratio 0.55; Q4 2025: ST Debt N/A, Current Ratio 1.15; [Source: SEC EDGAR XBRL] LongTermDebtMaturitiesRepaymentsOfPrincipalInNextTwelveMonths not disclosed"
    },
    {
      "finding": "FCF is deeply negative at approximately -$140M in Q1 2026 against CapEx of $390M, reflecting front-loaded HPC infrastructure spending with revenue recognition lagged — if $CRWV delays buildout ramp, CORZ has no disclosed contractual floor protecting cash outflows.",
      "evidence": "[Source: FMP /cash-flow-statement] Q1 2026: OCF $0.25B, CapEx -$0.39B, FCF -$0.14B; [Source: resolved counterparties] $CRWV = 100% customer concentration"
    },
    {
      "finding": "Insider selling is one-directional at -$9.59M net with zero buys across 15 Form 4 filings, with officer Todd Duchene executing repeated small-lot sales at $20-24 per share — below consensus analyst targets — suggesting internal price conviction may not match sell-side optimism.",
      "evidence": "[Source: FMP /insider-trading] Aggregate buys $0.00M vs sells $9.59M; most recent sales May 2026 at $21.53–$23.76/share vs analyst consensus target $31.60"
    },
    {
      "finding": "Operating margins are violently unstable — ranging from -1191% to +268% net margin across 8 quarters — driven by BTC price swings, impairment charges, and derivative revaluations, making any DCF-based valuation unreliable and the FMP intrinsic value of -$4.69 per share directionally meaningful.",
      "evidence": "[Source: FMP /income-statement] Q2 2024 NM -1191.4%, Q4 2024 NM +268.5%, Q3 2024 NM -180.8%; [Source: FMP DCF] intrinsic value -$4.69 vs market $27.20"
    }
  ],
  "analysis": "Core Scientific presents one of the most complex and elevated risk profiles in the digital infrastructure space, combining commodity price exposure, concentration risk, balance sheet leverage explosion, and execution uncertainty across a multi-billion dollar capex cycle simultaneously.\n\nThe most urgent risk is the debt structure. As of Q1 2026, CORZ carried $2.09B in convertible notes (the 3.00% 2029 and 0.00% 2031 Convertible Notes), alongside the $990M reclassified to short-term — a classification that appeared abruptly in Q1 with no prior quarters showing ST debt. This reclassification almost certainly reflects either a covenant breach triggering acceleration, a put feature on the convertibles becoming exercisable within 12 months, or a waiver condition. The XBRL dataset explicitly does not disclose maturity schedules, weighted average interest rates, or covenant thresholds, which is a critical transparency failure. Compounding this, in May 2026 — after Q1 close — Core Scientific Finance issued $3.30B in Secured Notes, bringing total disclosed debt to approximately $5.39B against Q1 cash of only $1.01B. Even if the new notes refinanced the $990M facility, the net leverage expansion is staggering for a company generating $120M in annual revenue from its legacy mining business. The change-of-control put provisions in all instruments create an M&A deterrent that limits strategic optionality precisely when it may be most needed.\n\nConcentration risk is binary. $CRWV represents 100% of CORZ's HPC/AI colocation customer base per resolved counterparty data. There is no disclosed revenue floor, take-or-pay minimum, or penalty schedule if CoreWeave delays capacity uptake. CORZ is simultaneously a construction contractor (capital at risk during buildout) and a facilities operator (revenue dependent on CoreWeave's own GPU demand and financial health). CoreWeave itself recently went public and carries its own capital market risks. A CoreWeave revenue disruption — delayed ramp, contract renegotiation, or financial distress — would eliminate CORZ's growth thesis and expose the debt service capacity of $5.39B in obligations.\n\nBitcoin mining as a backstop is insufficient. The mining segment generated approximately $120M in Q1 2026 revenue at ~25% gross margin, or $30M gross profit. At sustained BTC prices below $70-75K (vs ~$103K at time of filings), mining economics compress rapidly given the post-halving environment. The mining segment's OCF contribution is insufficient to service the debt stack independently, yet management has not disclosed specific BTC price floors or contingency CapEx curtailment thresholds.\n\nFCF burn of -$140M per quarter against a $1.01B cash balance implies approximately 7 quarters of runway if CapEx continues at Q1 2026 pace and the $3.30B note proceeds are earmarked for HPC infrastructure rather than liquidity. The actual runway depends entirely on deployment pace for the HPC buildout and when CoreWeave begins paying contractual revenue — data that is not publicly available.\n\nMacro context adds pressure: 10-year treasury at 4.5% and rising makes the cost of any refinancing expensive, while slowing GDP (2.0% in Q1 2026 after a near-contraction) and elevated CPI reduce risk appetite for speculative infrastructure credits. The beta of 5.45 means equity volatility will be extreme around any BTC price move or debt market event.\n\nAnalyst consensus of 19 Buy ratings with a $31.60 average target appears to price in successful HPC ramp execution with no debt restructuring — a scenario that is plausible but requires flawless execution of an unprecedented capital deployment alongside a single counterparty. The risk/reward is asymmetric to the downside given the leverage profile.",
  "data_gaps": [
    "Exact maturity dates, interest rates, covenant thresholds, and lender identities for the $990M ST debt reclassification and the $3.30B May 2026 Secured Notes are not disclosed in XBRL or available filing excerpts — this is the single most critical unknown for solvency assessment",
    "Binding HPC/AI colocation revenue backlog, take-or-pay minimum guarantees, and CORZ penalty exposure for delayed capacity delivery under the CoreWeave contract(s) are not publicly disclosed",
    "Bitcoin price break-even for the mining segment post-April 2024 halving given current fleet efficiency (J/TH metrics) is not quantified in available data, preventing modeling of mining cash flow at stress BTC prices"
  ],
  "questions": [
    {
      "question_text": "What are the exact maturity date, interest rate, covenant triggers, and lender identity of the $990M facility reclassified to short-term debt in Q1 2026, and were the May 2026 $3.30B Secured Notes proceeds specifically used to retire this obligation or is it still outstanding and accelerating?",
      "priority": 1,
      "auto_answerable": false
    },
    {
      "question_text": "What is the total contractually committed revenue from CoreWeave under binding take-or-pay provisions as of Q1 2026, and what specific financial penalty does CORZ face — or absorb — if CoreWeave delays capacity uptake by 6-12 months beyond the agreed buildout schedule?",
      "priority": 1,
      "auto_answerable": false
    },
    {
      "question_text": "At what sustained BTC price level does the mining segment generate negative operating cash flow given current fleet hash rate and efficiency, and has management disclosed any pre-committed CapEx curtailment threshold or asset monetization plan if BTC sustains below $70K for more than one quarter?",
      "priority": 1,
      "auto_answerable": false
    }
  ]
}

Business Quality.content:
{
  "score": 42,
  "score_rationale": "CORZ has nascent HPC/AI colocation optionality via its $CRWV relationship and improving revenue trajectory, but endemic margin volatility, negative equity, ~$2B debt load, persistent free cash flow deficits, and a commoditized self-mining core business prevent a quality score above the low-40s.",
  "key_findings": [
    {
      "finding": "Gross margins have been wildly volatile — ranging from -0.2% to 27.5% over 8 quarters — reflecting near-total dependence on Bitcoin price and network difficulty rather than durable pricing power.",
      "evidence": "[Source: FMP /income-statement quarterly, Q2 2023–Q1 2025]"
    },
    {
      "finding": "Free cash flow has been consistently negative for all 8 quarters reported, running at -$0.09B to -$0.14B per quarter in the most recent periods as CapEx escalates to $0.39B in Q1 2025 alone.",
      "evidence": "[Source: FMP /cash-flow-statement quarterly, Q1 2025]"
    },
    {
      "finding": "A $990M short-term debt position appeared on the Q1 2025 balance sheet with no prior quarters showing ST debt, compressing the current ratio from 1.15 to 0.55 — the nature, maturity, and covenant structure of this facility remain unresolved and represent acute liquidity risk.",
      "evidence": "[Source: FMP /balance-sheet quarterly, Q1 2025 vs Q4 2024]"
    },
    {
      "finding": "$CRWV has referenced CORZ as a partner and counterparty in its own filings, suggesting a material HPC/AI colocation relationship, but no disclosed revenue backlog, take-or-pay structure, or margin contribution is available to quantify the business quality uplift.",
      "evidence": "[Source: Resolved counterparties — $CRWV mentions CORZ as partner (1) and other (2)]"
    },
    {
      "finding": "Insider activity shows zero buys versus $9.59M in sells over the last 15 Form 4 filings, all by the same officer, at prices well below current levels — directionally negative signal on management conviction.",
      "evidence": "[Source: FMP /insider-trading, 2026-05-06 through 2026-05-20]"
    }
  ],
  "analysis": "Core Scientific presents as a structurally challenged business masquerading as a quality compounder due to its HPC/AI narrative. The self-mining segment — which has historically dominated revenues — is a commodity business with no pricing power: its gross margin is a mathematical output of Bitcoin price, network hashrate difficulty, and power cost, not competitive positioning. Over eight quarters, gross margins swung from -0.2% (Q3 2023) to 27.5% (Q2 2023) and back to 25.8% (Q1 2025), confirming there is no stable earnings floor in the core business. [Source: FMP /income-statement quarterly]\n\nThe colocation/HPC pivot is the thesis-saving element. CORZ owns power infrastructure and large-format data center campuses in the U.S. that can be re-purposed for high-density compute workloads — a genuine physical moat in a supply-constrained AI infrastructure market. The $CRWV relationship is the most tangible evidence of this pivot gaining traction. [Source: Resolved counterparties — $CRWV] However, no public disclosure quantifies the revenue backlog, contract duration, take-or-pay structure, or margin profile of this relationship. Until those terms are disclosed, the HPC segment cannot be modeled with any rigor, and the quality upgrade thesis remains speculative.\n\nThe balance sheet is a serious concern. Q1 2025 shows $1.01B cash against $0.99B short-term debt and $1.06B long-term debt — total gross debt approaching $2B. Negative equity (D/E of -1.57) reflects cumulative losses that have wiped out stockholders' book value. The sudden emergence of $990M in short-term debt in Q1 2025, where no such balance existed in Q4 2024, is alarming and unexplained by the available data. If this is a revolving credit facility funding the $0.39B quarterly CapEx pace, the covenant risk and refinancing risk are material. The current ratio of 0.55 suggests CORZ cannot cover near-term obligations from liquid assets alone. [Source: FMP /balance-sheet quarterly]\n\nCapEx intensity is accelerating — $0.39B in Q1 2025 alone versus $0.12B-$0.27B in prior quarters — and is outrunning operating cash flow of $0.25B, producing -$0.14B FCF. At this burn rate and CapEx trajectory, the company will need either HPC contract monetization or additional capital raises to sustain operations. Analyst consensus is overwhelmingly bullish (19 Buys, 0 Sells), but this likely reflects the AI infrastructure optionality narrative rather than current fundamental quality. The FMP proprietary rating of C (2/5) is more consistent with the underlying financial reality. [Source: FMP /analyst-estimates, FMP rating]\n\nThe ROA of -39.6% on a TTM basis underscores that asset utilization is deeply inefficient at current earnings levels. Revenue growth of +44.9% YoY in Q1 2025 is encouraging but accompanied by a -301.3% net margin, suggesting the growth is being subsidized rather than earned. [Source: FMP /income-statement quarterly, Q1 2025] In sum: CORZ has real physical infrastructure assets and a credible HPC pivot story, but current business quality is poor — margins are thin and cyclical, the balance sheet is stressed, FCF is negative, and the quality upgrade is entirely contingent on HPC contract execution that has not yet been financially demonstrated.",
  "data_gaps": [
    "The nature, maturity, covenants, and draw conditions of the $990M short-term debt facility that appeared in Q1 2025 are undisclosed — this is the single most important data gap for assessing going-concern risk.",
    "No public disclosure of the $CRWV HPC/AI colocation contract terms — backlog size, duration, take-or-pay structure, margin contribution — making it impossible to quantify the quality of the pivot business.",
    "Bitcoin price sensitivity analysis and the specific network difficulty/BTC price breakeven for self-mining gross margin positivity are not provided, preventing stress-testing of the core segment's floor."
  ],
  "questions": [
    {
      "question_text": "What is the exact structure, maturity date, drawn amount, and financial covenants of the $990M short-term debt facility on the Q1 2025 balance sheet — specifically, are there any conditions under which the lender can accelerate repayment or restrict CapEx?",
      "priority": 1,
      "auto_answerable": false
    },
    {
      "question_text": "What are the contractual terms of the CORZ/$CRWV HPC colocation arrangement — specifically the total contracted revenue value, contract duration, take-or-pay provisions, and the gross margin profile of this segment relative to self-mining?",
      "priority": 1,
      "auto_answerable": false
    },
    {
      "question_text": "At what blended Bitcoin price and network difficulty level does CORZ's self-mining gross margin turn negative given current power costs and fleet efficiency, and what hedging or power purchase agreements exist to protect the downside?",
      "priority": 2,
      "auto_answerable": false
    }
  ]
}

Financial Health.content:
{
  "score": 38,
  "score_rationale": "CORZ's financial health is severely impaired by the $990M short-term debt reclassification against only $1.01B cash, a negative equity base, persistent net losses, and deeply negative FCF — the HPC pivot thesis requires near-perfect execution on refinancing and contract ramp simultaneously, with negligible margin for error.",
  "key_findings": [
    {
      "finding": "A $990M debt tranche was reclassified to short-term in Q1 2026, creating an acute liquidity cliff: cash coverage is effectively 1.02x with zero buffer after accounting for ongoing FCF burn of ~$140M/quarter.",
      "evidence": "[Source: FMP /balance-sheet-statement] Q1 2026: Cash $1.01B vs ST Debt $0.99B; FCF Q1 2026: -$0.14B"
    },
    {
      "finding": "Equity is deeply negative (D/E of -1.57x in Q1 2026), meaning the company is technically insolvent on a book value basis and cannot access most conventional secured lending without subordinating existing creditors.",
      "evidence": "[Source: FMP /balance-sheet-statement] Q1 2026 D/E ratio -1.57; LT Debt $1.06B persists alongside negative equity"
    },
    {
      "finding": "Operating cash flow has improved sequentially ($0.03B → $0.13B → $0.15B → $0.25B), but CapEx is accelerating sharply ($0.39B in Q1 2026 alone), keeping FCF deeply negative and signaling the HPC buildout is consuming cash faster than the core business generates it.",
      "evidence": "[Source: FMP /cash-flow-statement] Q1 2026: OCF $0.25B, CapEx -$0.39B, FCF -$0.14B; Q4 2025: OCF $0.15B, CapEx -$0.27B"
    },
    {
      "finding": "Gross margins have recovered to ~25-26% in Q4 2025 and Q1 2026 from near-zero in 2024, driven by Bitcoin price appreciation and the HPC colocation revenue ramp, but net margins remain wildly volatile (-301% in Q1 2026) due to non-cash charges and debt-related items.",
      "evidence": "[Source: FMP /income-statement] Q1 2026 GM 25.8%, NM -301.3%; Q3 2024 GM 4.7%, NM -180.8%"
    },
    {
      "finding": "Analyst consensus is overwhelmingly bullish (19 Buy, 3 Hold, 0 Sell) yet the DCF intrinsic value is deeply negative (-$4.69 per share vs. current $27.20), highlighting that the bull case is entirely predicated on uncontracted future HPC cash flows and successful debt refinancing — neither of which is confirmed in the available data.",
      "evidence": "[Source: FMP /analyst-estimates, /dcf] DCF IV: -$4.69; Analyst avg PT $31.60; 22 analysts covering"
    }
